---
/**
 * Fender Stratocaster dating guide. FIRST OF THE MODEL-AXIS SPOKES.
 *
 * The Fender cluster's method axis is finished (pot codes, neck dates, body
 * dates, pickup dates, physical features). Those pages are organized BY METHOD,
 * across every model. This page opens the second axis: one model, read year by
 * year. The payload is the 1954-to-1971 Stratocaster spec table, which is the
 * one thing neither the parts page (organized by part) nor the year-specific
 * blog authentication guides (one year, deep) provide.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY SHORT ON SERIALS. Fender serial numbers are not model specific:
 * a 1963 neck-plate serial reads the same under a Strat, a Jazzmaster, or a
 * Jazz Bass. Restating the hub's serial charts here would be a near-duplicate
 * of /fender-guitars-serial-number-guide/ and would compete with it for the
 * head term. The serial section therefore names the three plate eras, shows
 * what each looks like, and sends the reader up to the hub for the charts.
 * VG-0066 / VG-0067 / VG-0068 are all flagged KEEP FLAGGED in VG_FACTS on their
 * numeric ranges (sources overlap and disagree), so NO year-to-serial-number
 * table is published here. That is on purpose, not an omission.
 *
 * Content facts trace to Joe's own published pages and to VG_FACTS.md
 * (joes-vintage-databases): VG-0053 (dating methodology), VG-0056 and VG-0057
 * (pickup dating and era), VG-0066 / VG-0067 / VG-0068 (serial eras, ranges not
 * asserted), VG-0070 (maple to slab to veneer), VG-0071 (CBS-era tells: large
 * headstock late 1965, block inlays circa 1966, 3-bolt 1971), VG-0072 (pre-CBS
 * definition and the gradual 1965 cutover), VG-0073 and VG-0074 (custom color
 * and refinish value).
 *
 * On-site sources for the year rows, all already reviewed and live:
 * fender-physical-features.astro (logo, board, dots, guards, saddles, tuners,
 * string trees), fender-pot-codes.astro (supplier eras, capacitor table, the
 * 1954 100K-to-250K split-shaft change), fender-pickup-dates.astro (Formvar to
 * plain enamel in 1964, black to grey bobbins, Strat poles staggered from 1954,
 * Alnico 3 on the earliest Strat pickups with Alnico 5 becoming the standard),
 * fender-neck-dates.astro (the Stratocaster model code running 2, then 13, then
 * 22), and the 1962 / 1963 / 1966 / 1967 Stratocaster authentication guides.
 *
 * NOT YET IN VG_FACTS, flagged for a joes-vintage-databases PR: the Stratocaster
 * neck-stamp model-code sequence (2 through 1965, 13 from 1966 to 1968, 22 from
 * 1968 to about 1973); the August 1962 slab-to-veneer month; the 1958 two-color
 * to three-color sunburst change; the mid-1956 round-guide to butterfly string
 * tree change; and the 1954 100K solid-shaft to 250K split-shaft pot change.
 *
 * CLAIMS DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED. (a) An Alnico 3 to Alnico 5 changeover year:
 * the pickup-dates page states the change without a date because the sources do
 * not agree, and this page matches that wording rather than inventing "circa
 * 1956". (b) Any numeric serial-range-to-year table, per the flags above.
 * (c) A single confident year for the Kluson single-line to double-line change,
 * which fender-physical-features.astro documents as genuinely disputed.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import LicensedImage from "../components/primitives/LicensedImage.astro";
import { resolveImage, assetSrc, ogCardSize } from "../lib/images";
import { contact, hrefs, SITE_URL } from "../config/site";

const meta = (file: string) => resolveImage("images/fender-sn/" + file)!;
const bmeta = (slug: string, file: string) => resolveImage(`images/blog/${slug}/${file}`)!;

const G62 = "1962-fender-stratocaster-authentication-guide";
const G63 = "1963-fender-stratocaster-authentication-guide";
const G66 = "1966-fender-stratocaster-authentication-guide";
const G67 = "1967-fender-stratocaster-authentication-guide";

const PAGE_PATH = "/fender-stratocaster-dating-guide/";
const HUB_PATH = "/fender-guitars-serial-number-guide/";
const HUB_HREF = HUB_PATH;
const OG = `images/blog/${G62}/1962-fender-stratocaster-sunburst-8-scaled.jpg`;

// ---- Data ----
// Year-by-year. Only changes that show on the guitar, and only ones traceable
// to the sources listed in the header comment. Years with no headline change
// say so rather than inventing one.
const yearRows: [string, string, string][] = [
  [
    "1954",
    "Launch year. Single-ply white pickguard with eight screws, a one-piece maple neck with a skunk stripe down the back, a two-color sunburst, and a small round string guide. The very earliest examples carry the serial number stamped into the plastic tremolo cavity cover on the back rather than on the neck plate, which is a first-year-only quirk. The spring guitars went out with 100K solid-shaft pots and brittle polystyrene knobs that cracked, sometimes while being pushed on at the factory. By about September Fender had moved to 250K split-shaft pots and a knob redesigned with support spokes under the skirt.",
    "Count the pickguard screws. Eight is 1954 to 1959. Then turn the guitar over and look at the tremolo cover.",
  ],
  [
    "1955",
    "No headline change. A 1955 carries the same spec as a late 1954, so nothing on the outside separates the two.",
    "Nothing visual. Go straight to the neck stamp and the pots.",
  ],
  [
    "1956",
    "Alder becomes the standard body wood under sunburst and custom colors, chosen over ash for its tighter grain and better paint adhesion. Around the middle of the year the small round string guide gives way to the wider butterfly tree.",
    "A butterfly string tree on the headstock rather than the small round guide.",
  ],
  [
    "1957",
    "The paper-in-oil phone book tone capacitor changes from a brown body to a white one. Everything else holds.",
    "Lift the guard. A white phone book cap on the tone pot means 1957 or later.",
  ],
  [
    "1958",
    "The two-color sunburst gives way to the three-color, with an orange band between the yellow center and the dark edge. On Kluson tuners the PAT. APPLD. stamp under the housing gives way to a full patent number around now.",
    "Is there orange in the burst? No orange points to 1954 through 1957.",
  ],
  [
    "1959",
    "The biggest year on the list, and three changes land together. Around mid-year the one-piece maple neck gives way to a rosewood fingerboard, and the first of those boards are the thick flat-bottomed slabs. The single-ply guard becomes three-ply celluloid nitrate with eleven screws. The dots become clay.",
    "A rosewood board and eleven pickguard screws. A 1959 can honestly be either side of this.",
  ],
  [
    "1960",
    "No headline change. Worth knowing that some 1960 neck plates carry the serial along the bottom edge rather than in the middle, which is correct and makes people doubt an honest guitar.",
    "Nothing new to see. The neck stamp does the work this year.",
  ],
  [
    "1961",
    "The ceramic disc tone capacitor replaces the paper-in-oil phone book cap. Leftover paper caps still turn up in early 1962, which is a normal Fender overlap.",
    "A round ocher wafer on the tone pot instead of a flat waxed block.",
  ],
  [
    "1962",
    "Partway through August the slab rosewood board gives way to the thinner curved veneer. That single change is the sharpest value line inside the pre-CBS years. Right at the end of 1962 the L-prefix serials begin, so an L plate on a late 1962 build is possible.",
    "Look at the rosewood depth at the nut. A thick band is slab, a thin line is veneer.",
  ],
  [
    "1963",
    "The L-prefix neck plate is the norm, though some January and February guitars still wear the older five-digit numbers. During the year the two clay dots at the twelfth fret move from wide spacing to narrow, and both are correct for 1963 depending on the month.",
    "An L in front of the serial on the neck plate.",
  ],
  [
    "1964",
    "Two changes that get read wrong constantly. Around mid-year the thin spaghetti logo gives way to the thicker transition logo. Both are gold on a Stratocaster, so the tell is the weight of the script and not the color. Late in the year the clay dots on the face of the board give way to pearloid, while the small dots on the edge of the neck stay clay into 1965. The coil wire changes from Formvar to plain enamel, and the pickup bobbins begin moving from black to grey. The neck patent 3,143,028 is granted in August, so it appears on decals from then on.",
    "A noticeably thicker, bolder logo. Read it on a Strat only: a Telecaster kept the spaghetti logo about a year longer.",
  ],
  [
    "1965",
    "CBS completes the purchase in January, but the guitar changes in stages across the year rather than overnight. The pickguard material moves from celluloid nitrate to white plastic around mid-year, the big-F neck plate arrives around the third quarter, and the headstock enlarges late in the year.",
    "A large F stamped on the neck plate, or the wider headstock outline.",
  ],
  [
    "1966",
    "The large CBS headstock is effectively universal, and the handful of small-headstock 1966 guitars are leftover blanks being finished out. Grey-bottom pickups with plain enamel wire, double-line Klusons, and a three-ply white plastic guard that ages cream rather than mint.",
    "A big headstock with a gold transition logo, and a guard that never went green.",
  ],
  [
    "1967",
    "A maple cap fingerboard returns as an option around mid-year, glued on top rather than cut into the neck, so the back stays clean. Fender's own F-stamped tuners phase in across the year and are standard by 1968. The bold black CBS logo starts appearing late in the year.",
    "A maple board with no skunk stripe. That pairing is a late-1960s guitar, never a 1950s one.",
  ],
  [
    "1968",
    "The heavy black CBS logo is standard, polyester finish arrives, and the cloth-covered wire in the cavity gives way to plastic-insulated wire as the old stock runs out. A 1968 can honestly have either, or both. The green Nissei chicklet capacitor appears this year only.",
    "The thick black logo, drawn heavy enough to read on camera.",
  ],
  [
    "1969 to 1970",
    "Mostly settled. The tone capacitor drops from 0.1 to 0.05, so the tone control rolls off less. Maple cap necks are common alongside rosewood.",
    "Read the neck stamp. The Stratocaster model code changes inside this window.",
  ],
  [
    "1971",
    "The four-bolt neck plate gives way to the three-bolt Micro-Tilt plate, the skunk stripe returns with the bullet truss rod, and late in the year the stamped steel saddles give way to die-cast zinc.",
    "Count the bolts in the neck plate. Three means 1971 or later.",
  ],
];

// The five checks that place a Strat in an ERA before you touch a screwdriver.
// Per Joe 2026-08-08: features give a WINDOW, often a broader one than the serial
// does. They corroborate the serial rather than beating it. Only the neck heel
// date and the body date are genuinely tight. Nothing here may imply otherwise.
//
// HEADSTOCK ROW, per Joe: do NOT call the enlargement "Stratocaster-specific".
// Other Fender models took enlarged headstocks in the CBS years too. The
// Telecaster is the notable holdout, so this is a read-it-against-the-right-model
// check, not a Strat-only one. Same fix in the tip below, the headstock section,
// the FAQ, and fender-physical-features.astro.
//
// LOGO ROW, per Joe and confirmed against Joe's own two photos: on a STRATOCASTER
// the spaghetti logo is GOLD, not silver, and so is the transition logo. Color
// does not separate them, script weight does. (Note the standard Telecaster
// spaghetti logo IS described as silver on the 1959 Tele guide, and its Custom
// as gold, so this looks model-dependent. Only the Strat claim is corrected here.)
const fastRows: [string, string, string][] = [
  ["Headstock size", "Small through 1965, large from late 1965", "The one you can read from across a room. Read it against the right model though: the Telecaster headstock never grew, so this check does not carry over to a Tele."],
  ["The logo", "Spaghetti to mid-1964, transition mid-1964 to 1967, heavy black CBS from 1968", "On a Stratocaster the first two are both gold, so go by the weight of the script rather than the color. Spaghetti is thin and delicate, transition is thicker and bolder, CBS is heavy black."],
  ["The fingerboard", "Maple with a skunk stripe to mid-1959, slab rosewood to August 1962, veneer rosewood after, and a maple cap option back from mid-1967", "Look at the depth of the rosewood at the nut, and at the back of the neck for the stripe. Maple with no stripe is the late-1960s cap, not a 1950s neck."],
  ["The dots", "Clay to late 1964, pearloid after", "Clay is dull, matte, and usually discolored. Pearloid is bright and visibly swirled."],
  ["The pickguard", "One-ply with eight screws to 1959, three-ply nitrate with eleven to mid-1965, three-ply plastic after", "Count the screw holes in the body rather than in the guard. A guard can be swapped; the holes cannot."],
];

// Strat neck-heel model code. This is the most model-specific dating tool there
// is, and it currently sits buried inside the neck-dates method page.
const codeRows: [string, string][] = [
  ["2", "Through 1965"],
  ["13", "1966 to 1968"],
  ["22", "1968 to about 1973"],
];

// Serial plate eras. Named and shown, NOT charted. Charts live on the hub.
const plateRows: [string, string, string][] = [
  ["Plain chrome four-bolt plate", "1954 to late 1962", "Four or five digits, no letter. Fender pre-stamped plates in batches and pulled them off the stack in no order, so these overlap heavily year to year."],
  ["L-prefix plate", "Late 1962 to late 1965", "An L in front of five digits. The last of the pre-CBS plates."],
  ["Big-F plate", "About the third quarter of 1965 to 1976", "Six digits stamped above a large stylized F. The CBS plate."],
];

const tocItems: [string, string][] = [
  ["#start", "Start Here"],
  ["#fast", "The Five Fastest Checks"],
  ["#years", "Stratocaster Year By Year"],
  ["#headstock", "Headstock And Logo"],
  ["#neck", "The Neck And The Board"],
  ["#guard", "Pickguard And Pickups"],
  ["#heel", "The Neck Heel Date"],
  ["#body", "Body Dates"],
  ["#serial", "What The Serial Number Adds"],
  ["#limits", "What Features Can't Prove"],
  ["#cross", "Confirm It From The Inside"],
  ["#faq", "Common Questions"],
  ["#appraisal", "Free Expert Appraisal"],
  ["#about-joe", "About Joe"],
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What Year Is My Stratocaster?",
    a: "Work it in three passes and expect each one to give you a window rather than an answer. Read the headstock first, because the size and the logo style put the guitar in a five-year window from across the room. Then read the board, the dots, the pickguard screws, and the serial number together, treating the serial as one vote among several. Then open it up for the parts Fender actually dated: the neck heel stamp and the body date, backed up by the pot codes. Those last two are the only things on the guitar that will give you a month.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I Date A Stratocaster From The Serial Number Alone?",
    a: "No. Fender pre-stamped neck plates in large batches and assemblers grabbed them off the stack in no particular order, so a plate can sit for months before it goes on a guitar, and the published ranges overlap year to year and disagree between sources. That said, do not swing too far the other way. The features are not sharper than the serial, and several of them are blunter: a large headstock covers everything from late 1965 on. The serial is one vote, the features are more votes, and only the neck heel date and the body date will actually hand you a month.",
  },
  {
    q: "My Stratocaster Has A Big Headstock. What Year Does That Make It?",
    a: "Late 1965 or later. Fender widened the Stratocaster headstock late in 1965 and it stayed wide, so the large outline is a floor rather than a window. Two things to keep straight. The Stratocaster was not the only Fender to take a bigger headstock in the CBS years, so a large headstock is not by itself a Strat tell. And the Telecaster headstock kept its shape throughout, so this check does not transfer to a Tele at all.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is My Strat Pre-CBS?",
    a: "Pre-CBS means built before CBS completed the purchase in January 1965, but the guitars did not change on that date. Early and mid 1965 is a genuine grey area where a guitar can carry an L-series plate, a small headstock, and pre-CBS pickups while technically being a CBS-era instrument. Read the features rather than the calendar, and be careful of a seller who does the opposite.",
  },
  {
    q: "My Stratocaster Has A Maple Neck. Does That Make It A 1950s Guitar?",
    a: "Not on its own. Look at the back of the neck. A one-piece maple neck from the 1950s has to have a dark walnut skunk stripe down the back, because the truss rod went in from behind and the stripe caps the channel. The maple cap neck that returned around mid-1967 has a separate board glued on top, so the truss rod went in from the front and the back is clean unbroken maple. Same wood, same look from the playing side, and about a decade apart.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does A Five-Way Switch Mean My Strat Is Not Original?",
    a: "It means the switch was changed, and that is all. Fender fitted a three-way switch until 1977. Players had been balancing it between detents for the in-between sounds since the 1960s, and a great many vintage Strats got a five-way dropped in at some point. It costs originality and it is worth noting in a valuation, but it does not make the guitar a fake, and the original three-way often comes with it in the case pocket.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why Do My Pot Codes Read Older Than The Year On The Neck?",
    a: "Because that is the expected result, not a problem. A pot code dates the pot, and the guitar was assembled on or after that date. Pots slightly older than the build are exactly right. It gets more extreme on CBS-era guitars, where Fender took delivery of an enormous lot of CTS pots in the mid-1960s and pulled from it for about five years, so 1966 codes turn up routinely in guitars built through about 1971. A pot dated years newer than the rest of the guitar is the real red flag.",
  },
  {
    q: "Should I Take My Stratocaster Apart To Date It?",
    a: "Only if you have done it before. Almost everything on this page can be read with the guitar assembled, and that is deliberate. Vintage screws, plastics, and finishes strip and crack easily, and a cracked sixty-year-old pickguard costs far more than the answer is worth. If the outside checks leave you stuck between two years, send me photos before you reach for a screwdriver.",
  },
];

// ---- Schema ----
const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "How To Date A Fender Stratocaster, Year By Year",
